  simple: "The central highlands of present-day Shewa had once been the political centre of the Christian Kingdom. In the 16th century the court moved to the Lake Tana area because of the pressure from Adal and the Oromo expansion, and Shewa was left isolated and disunited, with several local chiefs. Strong Gondarine rulers such as Iyassu I (r. 1682-1706) tried to control Shewa by sending military expeditions, but it gradually detached itself. The Shewan kingdom emerged in the area of Manz around the end of the 17th or the beginning of the 18th century, founded by a local chief called Negasi. During the Zemene-Mesafint its rulers stayed out of the northern wars and consolidated the kingdom with its centre at Ankober. It reached its heyday under Nigus Sahle Sellase (r. 1813-1847), who kept law and order and opened relations with Britain and France.",
  detailed: "<p><b>Shewa as the old centre.</b> As was shown in an earlier unit, the central highlands of the present-day Shewa had been the political centre of the Christian Kingdom. However, the Christian state shifted its capital to the Lake Tana area as a result of the pressure from Adal and the Oromo expansion of the 16th century.</p><p><b>Gondarine attempts to control Shewa.</b> Some strong Gondarine rulers, including Iyassu I (r. 1682-1706), tried to control Shewa and to that end sent military expeditions there. Even so, Shewa gradually detached itself from the newly founded centre of the Christian state. During this period of isolation Shewa was disunited, and several local chiefs emerged in various parts of the region.</p><p><b>The rise of the Shewan kingdom.</b> The Shewan kingdom nevertheless emerged in the area of Manz, around the end of the 17th century or the beginning of the 18th century. The founder of the kingdom was a local chief known as Negasi.</p><p><b>Isolation as an advantage.</b> During the Zemene-Mesafint, the successive rulers of Shewa safely isolated themselves from the wars that were tearing northern Ethiopia apart. That distance from the fighting allowed them to consolidate the Shewan kingdom with their centre at Ankober, instead of exhausting their strength in the warlords' struggles.</p><p><b>The heyday under Sahle Sellase.</b> The kingdom reached its heyday under Nigus Sahle Sellase (r. 1813-1847). He brought several regions under Shewan control and maintained law and order in the kingdom.</p><p><b>Stability and foreign relations.</b> The stability that Sahle Sellase maintained enabled several foreign travellers to come and visit Shewa. Those visits in turn helped Sahle Sellase to establish independent foreign relations with Britain and France, giving Shewa a diplomatic standing that the warring northern lords did not have.</p>",
